<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><img id="imagen" src="2_Partner_files/partnertrail02.jpg" width="90%" onerror="this.style.display='none'" style="display: none;">                                  <h3 style="margin-top:14.05pt;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:14.05pt;
margin-left:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;
font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Aptos;mso-bidi-font-family:
Aptos;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">What</span></b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></h3><h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.5; margin-bottom: 12px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: Roboto, sans-serif;">

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin: 12pt 0cm;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 116%; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;">In the TRAIL, we define transdisciplinarity as
"the collaboration of academic researchers from various disciplines with
non-academic partners to jointly create new knowledge and theories for a common
goal" (OECD, 2021). This definition highlights three characteristics: 1)
non-academic participants collaborate with students, teachers, and researchers;
2) the collaboration is based on equality among all team members; 3) and the
focus revolves around a complex societal issue.<o:p></o:p></span></p>

<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin: 12pt 0cm;"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 116%; font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;">Transdisciplinary education thus means
integrating the knowledge and experience of researchers, teachers, and students
from different disciplines with non-academic participants: you, the societal
partner. Together, you co-create new knowledge or products to achieve a common
goal.</span></p></h2></body></html>